-var VERSION = "1.50.0"; /* 1.50.0 OUTREACH-ASK-FACTS-1 (2026-10-19, agent_issues 100097, pillar reach): the Ask panel and the command line can say what outreach needs contact. Asked "what outreach needs contact" they answered that their CONTEXT had no outreach queue, because it carried the queue, decision, system and business facts and nothing from qnfo-audit.outreach_queue. The explicit question is now answered from the table without the model (intent outreach-needs-contact), any question about outreach carries the counts by status and the waiting papers (arXiv id and title) first in CONTEXT, and the no-model fallback prints them. The address and author columns are never read; a read error is reported as unreadable, not as an empty queue. */ /* 1.49.2 FLEET-TICK-ISOLATE-1 (2026-10-19, agent_issues 2600, pillar autonomy): the fallback fleet_tick producer retries a failed insert once with OR IGNORE under the source cron:qnfo-fleet-dashboard:isolated, as qnfo-code-orchestrator 0.18.30 does. A constraint violation inside one D1 fixer trigger rolled the tick back for 144 minutes on 2026-10-18 (12:14Z-14:38Z) and the fallback, running the same insert through the same triggers, failed with it; now the tick commits and only the violating statement is skipped. fleet_heartbeat records ok 0 only when both attempts threw. */ /* 1.49.1 ARCH-SELF-MODEL-1 follow-up (2026-10-19, pillar autonomy): the live model listed three workers no layer named (ai-health-prober, fleet-exec, fleet-graph) as unclassified; the first two join the control plane and fleet-graph joins observe. */ /* 1.49.0 DASHBOARD-COST-FEED-1 (2026-10-19, agent_issues 100071, owner directive FULL-PLANE-ACCESS-1, pillar cost): GET /api/cost, the cost area's own read: 30-day run-rate by source (v_cost_run_rate), 14 days of daily spend (cost_daily), Workers AI calls, errors, tokens, neurons and estimated USD by model and by worker over 7 days (ai_call_counters), and every spend budget with headroom (fleet_budget); registered in API_INDEX under area cost, so /api/area/cost lists two feeds. cost-feed.test.mjs. */ /* 1.48.0 ARCH-SELF-MODEL-1 (2026-10-19, pillar autonomy; owner directive 2026-10-19: "the system needs to know more about its own architecture (self reference)"): the fleet can now be asked what it is. GET /api/architecture (and /api/architecture/worker/<name>, /table/<name>, /impact/<worker>, ?q=, ?full=1) and the page /architecture answer from the fleet code and live topology graph (FLEET-GRAPH-1, qnfo-graph), the service registry, the heartbeats and the cron table: every worker with its layer, purpose, version, last tick, schedules, routes, databases, service dependencies in both directions and the tables its code reads and writes; the databases, the fleet-exec loops, how a change travels from a work claim to a canaried deploy, the lanes of authority, the registries that hold the fleet's memory, the graph's own findings, and self: the sources it read, the ones it could not, and what it cannot see. Platform area page "Architecture". Open and read-only; names and counts only. */ /* 1.47.0 CREDIT-HALT-ONLY-1 (2026-10-19, owner directive): "AI credit runs out in about N days" is never a queue card and never urgent. The prepaid balance is topped up automatically, so a low balance halts nothing. The only credit card is a balance at or below zero AND gateway calls actually failing on credit in the last 6 hours (llm_gateway_log 402/403 or insufficient/quota/payment/billing/credit errors). A runway is display context only. */ /* 1.46.1 GA4-PROPERTY-MISMATCH-1 (2026-10-19, pillar reach, agent_issues 2935, code task ct_im2935): ga4Ingest read the default property 486253526, which holds 1-2 page views a day for qnfo-publications.pages.dev, while the Zaraz tag G-LV7RHRVW6R on qnfo.org, q08.org, qwav.tech, ipatent.me and empoweringchange.today sits in another property. With no pinned ops_config ga4_property_id it now lists the properties the service account can read (Analytics Admin accountSummaries), reads the web data streams of each, takes the property whose stream carries the measurement id, and saves it as ga4_property_id; when none does, the ledger row ga4-ingest-<day> says so (property_warning) and the default is read. The codeagent draft looked for measurementId on the property summary, which has none, and sat behind a numeric check that was always true. */ /* 1.46.0 KPI-THRESHOLDS-1 + CODE-FLOW-PR-SPLIT-1 + OWNER-REQUEST-RECEIPT-1 (2026-10-19, pillar autonomy; agent_issues 2727 and 2941, and the owner-queue metrics owner_queue_fleet_cards_open and owner_dispatched_items_7d): (1) the Metrics tab opens with Service levels: every registered metric against its threshold with a state (BREACH, WARN, NO_READING, OK, NO_THRESHOLD), the time in breach and its area, BREACH first and longest first, and the items past their SLA by class with the age of the oldest (v_metric_trigger_state, metric_registry, metric_history, v_sla_over_120m); GET /api/kpi gains service_levels and sla and loses every last_7d and all_time key (code tasks are one 30-day split, posts per channel 30 days). (2) The Code loop flow splits "closed, unmerged" by what happened to the pull request (code_task_pr_links): closed never opened a PR, PR closed unmerged, and superseded, which stays out of the failure share; one SQL (CODE_OUTCOME_SQL) feeds the flow and the Metrics tab. (3) The card the owner_request_requires_card trigger writes when an owner-request issue closes (source owner-request-issue) is a receipt: listed apart as Closed for you with its issue number, never in the count, the banner or an area queue; the gate already grades such a card "notice" (OWNER-NOTICE-GATE-1, migrations/2026-10-19-owner-notice-gate.sql), so the two metrics do not count it; this release changes only the page. */ /* 1.45.0 AREA-PAGES-1 (2026-10-19, pillar autonomy; owner directive 2026-10-18 "refactor the fleet dashboard and API feeds by functional area", repeated 2026-10-19 as "REFACTOR FLEET DASHBOARD BY FUNCTIONAL AREA"): the page half of AREA-FEEDS-1. (1) AREA_REGISTRY is the one table for the six areas; FEED_AREAS, AREA_DEFS, AREA_STARS, AREA_ROOT and AREA_FLOW are derived from it, and it holds what each area's page, tabs, cards, switches and owner-queue cards are. (2) The navigation is the areas (Overview, Cost, Research, Reach, Autonomy, Reliability, Platform, Command line); Metrics, Content and Changelog sit under their area behind a sub-bar. (3) /area/<id> answers for all six areas (/area/platform was a 404): the area's queue cards, constraint and breaches, pipelines, the cards that sat on the overview (Keep investing? under Cost, Progress and the social switch under Reach, System health under Reliability, the owner-queue switch under Autonomy), its reads with their lag, and a live refresh (?frag=1&rev=, 204 while nothing moved). The overview keeps the queue, one row per area (a named bottleneck or a stopped switch shows on the row) and the two headline cards. (4) GET /api by_area and /api/area/<id> name each area's page. (5) AREAS2_CSS was defined and never sent, so the per-area rows, the switch list and the summary link shipped unstyled on 1.44.5; it is in the page head now. (6) /kpi and /api/kpi answered 500 on 1.44.5 (a half-built KPI-THRESHOLDS-1 rebuilt the KPI document as if it were a map of metrics); the route serves the document as computed, and agent_issues 2727 stays open. (7) "AREA-MAP <id>:" banners mark 40 regions of this file by area (README, Code map). Additive except the overview's Pipelines, Progress and Pipeline controls cards, which moved to their areas. */ /* 1.44.7 FALLBACK-HEARTBEAT-PROOF-1 (2026-10-19, pillar autonomy): each scheduled run writes fleet_heartbeat (ok = the guarded fleet_tick fallback insert ran without error), so fm-single-heartbeat proves the second heartbeat producer is alive on a day the primary never misses instead of failing 24 hours after the last fallback tick. */ /* 1.44.4 AREA-FEEDS-5 (2026-10-18, pillar autonomy; deployment test of 1.44.3): GET /api was the one read that carried an ETag but no body `revision` and no ?since=, so a client that cannot send a header - the reason ?since= exists - could not dedupe the 13 KB index, and the index's own note ("a read with ?since=<revision> answers {not_modified:true}") was false about itself. It now goes through rtRead like every other read: body revision, ?since=, and an ETag over the content (endpoints plus the area grouping) instead of a path-and-schema string. The catalogue row now says "public 15s, ETag". Additive: no field removed, the ETag is strictly stronger. */ /* 1.44.3 AREA-FEEDS-4 (2026-10-18, pillar autonomy; closeout review of 1.44.2): the area feed omitted `blind`, the list of sources areasModel could not read, while /api/areas carries it. With a table unreadable and no breach rows the feed would have answered breaches:0 and status ok - a false green in the one read whose job is truthful state, which is exactly what AREAS-1 forbids ("an unreadable source is named in blind, never shown as zero"). The feed now carries blind (the same list /api/areas sends) and degraded:true when it is non-empty. Additive only: no field removed, status still agrees with /api/areas so the two cannot disagree. */ /* 1.44.2 AREA-FEEDS-3 (2026-10-18, pillar autonomy; red-team of the live 1.44.1): GET /api/area/<id> answered 500 {"error":"URI malformed"} for a malformed percent sequence (/api/area/%E0%A4%A, /api/area/%C0%80) because decodeURIComponent throws and the id was decoded unguarded, while every pre-existing read compares the raw path and answers 404. An anonymous poller can send one by accident, so a 500 here is noise in the fleet's own error accounting. The decode is now guarded and an unreadable escape falls back to the raw segment, which takes the 404 path. Everything else red-teamed clean: trailing slash, case, empty and nested ids, ?since= with and without the rev: prefix, ?full=1 size, the revision holding steady while data.age_min moved 22 -> 23 over 65 s (the nested clock field really is excluded), and no owner field in the body. No route or field removed. */ /* 1.44.1 AREA-FEEDS-2 (2026-10-18, pillar autonomy; two defects found by verifying 1.44.0 in production, both mine): (a) the page claim was wrong - humanHtml renders areas through focusRowsHtml, not areaSectionsHtml, which is dead code with no call site, so editing it changed nothing anyone sees; that edit is reverted and the link that was actually missing (an area row to its machine feed) is added to focusRowsHtml in both branches, clean areas included. (b) by_area[].live meant "every member read is live" while the feed's own live:true means "this feed is computed at request time"; two meanings under one name in one response, so the grouping field is renamed all_members_live and the feed keeps live. No route, field or behaviour removed. */ /* 1.44.0 AREA-FEEDS-1 (2026-10-18, owner directive "REFACTOR FLEET DASHBOARD AND API FEEDS BY FUNCTIONAL AREA. FEEDS SHALL ALWAYS BE UPDATED AUTOMATICALLY FROM LIVE DATA AND REAL-TIME"): the read surface is now organised by functional area, as data, not as a convention. (a) Every row of API_INDEX names the one functional area it serves (cost, research, reach, autonomy, reliability, platform), whether it is computed live at request time or from the rebuilt fleet state, and the worst-case lag a client must assume (source, lag_s). GET /api returns that grouping as areas + by_area, so a client can enumerate an area's feeds instead of guessing. (b) GET /api/area/<id> is one functional area as its own feed: its star metric and trend, its breaches ranked, the constraint it is believed to be stuck behind, its pipelines, and the reads that belong to it with their declared lag. It is computed from D1 at request time and revalidates like every other read. (c) Fixed a shadowed route: GET /api/ga4 answered 500 "GA4 property ID not set" live because an older handler for the same path stood in front of the GA4-DATA-API-1 ledger read and called the retired stub helpers; the stale handler and its two dead helpers are removed, so the ledger read answers and revalidates. (d) A snapshot-backed read now refreshes itself: /api/actions and /api/integration read fleet_dashboard_state without ever scheduling a rebuild (only /api/state did), so a poller could keep receiving a 15-minute-old answer; both go through freshState, and /health states which reads are snapshot-backed and which are live. (e) the area feed states the age of the oldest metric it reports (data.age_min) because those metrics are refreshed by other workers and on 2026-10-18 ranged from minutes to 18 hours; RT_CLOCK strips age_min at any depth, so the age never moves the revision while the values and the count do. No route removed, no field removed. */ /* 1.43.2 ALL-READS-1 (2026-10-18, pillar autonomy; owner directive "FIX ALL AUTOMATICALLY"): the last two open reads join the same contract, so every open read now revalidates. GET /api/loop and GET /api/changes kept returning no-store with no validator; both now go through rtRead and carry a revision, an ETag and ?since= (on /api/changes ?since= still means the timestamp it always meant: a revision is eight hex characters and can never equal an ISO date, so the two meanings cannot collide). With this the whole open read surface - 18 endpoints plus /health - answers the same way. */ /* 1.43.1 KPI-CLOCK-1 (2026-10-18, pillar autonomy; COMPACT-READS-1 follow-up, found by verifying the deploy): /api/kpi answered 200 instead of 304 because its body carries as_of and activity_as_of, built from Date.now() on every build, so two isolates (or one cache expiry) could never agree on a revision. The route suite missed it because kpiData hands back an isolate-cached object, so both calls in one process saw the same as_of; rt-api.test.mjs now slices the digest block and asserts a clock field never moves the revision and a content field always does (J2-J6), which fails without this fix. Both fields join RT_CLOCK. No behaviour change beyond the 304. */ /* 1.43.0 COMPACT-READS-1 (2026-10-18, pillar autonomy; owner directive "FIX ALL AUTOMATICALLY" after the RT-API-1 report): the three items that report listed as deliberately not done or as a sharp edge. (a) GET /api/areas was 101 KB and 55 KB of it was fullLever, the same text the clipped lever already carries at ten times the length; the API now sends the compact row and keeps the whole source text behind ?full=1, schema_version fleet-areas/v3 (the page is untouched: it renders from areasModel, not from the API). (b) GET /api/changelog sent all 267 releases (164 KB) to every poller that wanted the newest ten; it is now one page of 20 with ?limit=1..200, ?offset= and ?limit=all, schema_version changelog/v2, and it revalidates like the other reads instead of no-store. (c) the digest no longer strips fields by a blanket name rule: RT_CLOCK is the explicit list of clock fields and everything else is part of the identity, and rt-api.test.mjs now mutates a NON-clock field of every revalidating read and requires the ETag to move, so over-stripping fails the suite instead of silently serving a stale 304. (d) ?since=<revision> is now on /api/areas, /api/human and /api/changelog as well as /api/live, every revalidating read returns X-Revision, and a body field `revision`, so a client that cannot send a header can still dedupe. No route removed; no field removed without a parameter that restores it. */ /* 1.42.1 RT-API-FRAGMENT-REV-1 (2026-10-18, pillar autonomy; RT-API-1 follow-up, found by verifying the deploy): the first deploy revalidated the JSON reads exactly as designed (/api/live, /api/areas, /api/human answered 304 with no body) but the page fragment did not: the same code path sets an ETag and the edge serves X-Generated-At while the ETag never arrives on text/html, so the poll's If-None-Match could not match and it kept re-downloading 12 KB. The fragment now carries X-Content-Revision and accepts it back as ?rev=, answering 204 with no body while nothing has changed; If-None-Match and 304 remain honoured. No other route is touched. */ /* 1.42.0 RT-API-1 (2026-10-18, pillar autonomy; owner directive "REFACTOR FLEET DASHBOARD AND REAL-TIME APIS"): measured on the live worker before the change, no read answered with an ETag, every read answered Cache-Control: no-store and GET /api was a 404, so a poller could neither ask "did anything change?" nor enumerate what it may call; /api/areas alone was 122 KB and the page re-downloaded and re-rendered its fragment every 60 s whether or not anything had moved. Now (a) GET /api is the read API's own index: every public read with its auth, schema_version, cache policy and what makes it change (API_INDEX, one table instead of scattered knowledge); (b) GET /api/live is one consolidated snapshot for pollers (state verdict and probe counts, queue open/urgent/oldest, the five areas with their constraint and lever, the invest verdict, a content revision) so a poll is one call instead of four; (c) a read carries a weak ETag computed over its CONTENT digest (time-derived fields excluded, or nothing would ever revalidate), honours If-None-Match with 304 and an empty body, and states a cache policy: public reads may be reused 15 s, a response carrying owner fields never may (private, no-store, Vary on the credential); (d) /api/areas, /api/human and /api/live answer 304, and /api/live also accepts ?since=<revision> for clients that cannot send a header; (e) the page's 60 s poll sends the fragment's ETag and on 304 leaves the DOM untouched (no re-render, no lost focus or scroll); (f) /health advertises api-index, live-snapshot and conditional-reads. No route was removed and no payload field was dropped. */ /* 1.41.0 GA4-DATA-API-1 (2026-10-18, pillar reach): the GA4 stubs are replaced by a real Data API read (service account GA4_SA_KEY, RS256 JWT in WebCrypto) into reach_signals source ga4 once a day, ledger cloud_ops_events ga4-ingest-<day>, GET/POST /api/ga4, watchmaker op ga4-ingest; a refused grant writes its reason, never a zero. */ /* 1.40.1 WORKERS-BP-LOGGING-2 (2026-10-17, pillar security/observability): the request and cron error catches now log at error severity with structured JSON (they previously returned the error without logging, so unhandled request/cron failures were invisible in the logs); the remaining string-concatenated console.log/console.error calls are converted to structured JSON.stringify so logs are searchable. No control-flow changes. */ /* 1.40.0 REACH-DIRECT-TRAFFIC-1 (2026-10-17, pillar reach; owner directive: see which papers and links are accessed directly from Cloudflare analytics/logs, not just social-media clicks): the /kpi Reach section gains "Top papers, opened directly" and "Top links, opened directly" (7 days), read from reach_signals source cf-rum-human (Cloudflare Web Analytics RUM page loads, bots excluded) grouped per paper slug and per page, excluding the fleet's own surfaces so the owner's own browsing is not reader traffic. This is the direct-traffic complement to the social-post click counts under "Most popular posts". */ /* 1.39.2 OWNER-VOICE-CAP-PARITY-1 (2026-10-17, pillar reach, agent_issues 2729): portfolioWeeklyCap falls back to PORTFOLIO_SOCIAL_WEEKLY_CAP_DEFAULT = 21, the cap qnfo-social weeklyCap runs (PAPER-ROLLUP-1), instead of the literal 2; the 2 failed the cadence check on any normal week, so the 05:00Z guard wrote social_paused=1 on 10-15, 10-16 and 10-17. wrangler.toml sets SOCIAL_ALLOW_Q08=1 so the 1.38.3 q08-link rule does not flag links Q08-OPEN-1 made allowed. // 1.39.1 (2026-10-17): a lever is clipped on a word boundary, so it can no longer end mid-word ("about 4k neur..."), and the root-cause sentence names the titles it is expected to clear in quotes. */ /* 1.39.0 AREAS-2 (2026-10-17, pillar autonomy; owner directive EXECUTE REFACTOR: organise the dashboard by functional area and action, not by data source, without overload, extraneous information or redundancy): the 15-row breach wall and the five tiles are replaced by five constraint rows, each carrying the cost of its constraint, its owner, its lever, the lever type (code loop / your decision / fleet loops) and its remedy history; every other breach moves to a deterministic /area/<id> page, which replaces 15 per-row Diagnose model calls; AREA_ROOT promotes the known root cause (verification monoculture) over the tautological probe count and names the breaches it explains; a Pipeline controls card shows kill-switch state and when it was set; the duplicate Subscribers progress bar is removed; GET /api/areas carries schema_version fleet-areas/v2. */ /* 1.38.0 AREAS-1 (2026-10-17, pillar autonomy; owner request: refactor the dashboard around functional areas and action items, with bottlenecks and progress, without overload or redundancy): five area tiles (cost, research and content, reach and growth, autonomy loop, reliability and security) each with one star metric and its 14-day trend replace the four-number strip; breaching metrics are listed per area, worst three first (priority, then gap), each with owner, sparkline, direction and a Diagnose control that fills the command line; a Pipelines panel shows issue age, code loop, research queue and outreach with the bottleneck stage named; the Open data card and the business-case section are removed (every figure is a tile, a verdict reason or a sparkline); GET /api/areas. / 1.37.6 SCHEMA-ALTER-PROBE (2026-10-14, pillar core; the qnfo-ops 2.48.2 pattern, PR 1258): liveScheduled reads pragma_table_info(worker_schedules) and runs ALTER ... ADD COLUMN created_on only when the column is missing, so no failed D1 statement reaches Workers telemetry on each call; the schema block's outer catch logs. */ /* 1.37.5 WORKERS-BP-LOGGING-qnfo-fleet-dashboard-1 (2026-10-14, agent_issues 2532, pillar security): the 14 swallowed catches log; the nine server-side ones write console.error JSON with worker and where (the ALTER TABLE on worker_schedules skips the expected duplicate-column error), and the five in the browser scripts write console.debug. Control flow unchanged. */ /* 1.37.4 FLEET-JOURNEYS-WATCHMAKER-1 (2026-10-14, agent_issues 2554, pillar core): WATCHMAKER_OPS lists fleet-journeys, the qnfo-fleet-control journey tick (journey_runs runner cf, every 20 minutes). */ /* 1.37.3 SLA-120M (2026-10-14, owner directive: no SLA exceeds 120 minutes, 10-minute resolution): the loop-signal warn SLA is 120 min (was 720) and its breach comment speaks in minutes; the watchmaker stuck limits are in minutes (stuck_min) and every SLA-type limit is 120 min (inbound mail 72 h, code-loop PR green 6 h and waiting 48 h, attention decisions 240 h, transformation levers 24 h, q08 review and intents 48 h); lookback windows keep their length (Gmail runs and person merges 30 days, outreach-learner allocation 48 h). */ /* 1.37.2: the outreach-learner stuck count excludes jr-outreach-lookup-* runs, as it excludes jr-outreach-learner-*. */ /* 1.37.1 OUTREACH-LOOKUP-WATCHMAKER-1 (2026-10-14, pillar reach): WATCHMAKER_OPS lists outreach-lookup, the qnfo-cloud-ops email lookup that runs every 120 minutes (SLA-120M-3). */ /* 1.37.0 CONTENT-PIPELINE-1 (2026-10-14, pillars research and reach; owner directive "visibility onto the content creation process end-to-end on fleet dashboard"): a Content tab (/content, JSON /api/content, open) shows every idea, paper, revision, erratum and post on one production line from qnfo-audit v_content_pipeline (stage, state, time in stage, SLA verdict), with papers.qnfo.org release-gate verdicts (living-paper) and q08.org signal-to-piece gates (q08-signal); the cron writes metric papers_live_failing_release_gate hourly. */ /* 1.36.1 COMPAT-WATCHMAKER-1 (2026-10-13, agent_issues 2412, pillar autonomy): WATCHMAKER_OPS lists compat-exposure, the qnfo-ops worker_compat_state snapshot (cadence 12h). ATTENTION-STOP-GRADE-1 (agent_issues 2467): a STOP decision is graded on its prediction, the other social channels' pooled 7-day attention per item, not on its own channel, whose share it set to 0 (that read 0 -> 0, flat, for every STOP). */ /* 1.36.0 MAIL-REPLY-INSTRUCTION-1 (2026-10-13, pillar personal; owner screenshot "Fail."): "reply 984 always show me draft first before sending" was offered for sending as the email text. reply <id> <text> now sends the text as written only when it reads as an email (greeting, several lines or a sign-off); otherwise the text is the owner's instruction and the fleet writes a complete draft of that reply, shown first with Send reply. The Reply button on inbox cards and in mail <id> runs that draft directly. */ /* 1.35.0 MAIL-DRAFT-COMPLETE-1 (2026-10-13, pillar personal; owner: "when prompted the system must generate full, complete, ready-to-send drafts. do not burden the user with manual effort or input"): drafts no longer carry [[FILL]] gaps. The fleet reads the facts itself (the qnfo.org public profile, the owner identity document in the private store, and the GitHub record of any file or commit the thread names) and the model writes a complete reply, including any requested statement, from those records only; a sentence with a title, unit or placeholder no record supports is dropped in code; every draft carries Send reply. */ /* 1.34.0 MAIL-DRAFT-1 (2026-10-13, pillar personal; owner: "NEVER INVENT TITLES OR BUSINESS UNITS OR FABRICATE ANY OTHER INFORMATION ... REQUIRING MULTIPLE BACK-AND-FORTH PROMPTS"): a request to draft a reply no longer goes to the general question prompt. It finds the message by address, id or inbox card, gives the model the stored thread and only the owner's name, turns every fact on record nowhere into a [[FILL: ...]] gap (a title or unit the thread lacks is replaced in code), writes the sign-off in code (name and sending address), shows real line breaks, and offers Edit, then send (the full reply in the command line, up to 8000 characte